  'virtual-school-greece-after-gymnasio': {
    cat:'online-school', country:'greece',
    img:'linear-gradient(135deg,#0A1A14,#1E2F5F)', splash:'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1560703650-ef3e0f254ae0?w=1200&q=75',
    t:'Virtual School in Greece: The Window After Gymnasio — and the Islands the Campuses Never Reached',
    date:'August 2026 · 8 min read',
    author:'Brendaliz Chelangat', role:'Head of Academics',
    metaTitle:'Virtual School Greece 2026: The Optional Lykeio, the A-Level Window & the Island Gap — Smartious',
    metaDesc:'Lykeio is optional in Greece — the A-Level years after gymnasio sit entirely outside compulsory education, and almost nobody says so. What that window makes possible, how to plan toward it from the compulsory years, and why the islands are where a virtual school matters most.',
    intro:'Greek education law contains one sentence that changes everything for internationally minded families, and it hides in plain sight: upper-secondary school is optional. Compulsory education ends with gymnasio at about fifteen — and from that moment, a Greek teenager owes the system nothing. Most families never notice, because most students simply continue to lykeio and the Panhellenics. But for the family whose child is aiming at London, for the island family with no senior option worth the ferry, and for the student the Greek exam machine does not serve, the window after gymnasio is the most valuable planning fact in the country. This article is about that window — and about the islands, where a virtual school is not one option among several but the only complete one.',
    sections:[
      {h:'The window, stated exactly',p:'Greek compulsory education runs from four to about fifteen and ends with the Apolytirio Gymnasiou. Lykeio — general or vocational — is by law optional: an entitlement, not an obligation. A teenager who holds the gymnasio certificate may lawfully spend the next three years in full-time live Cambridge A-Levels, with no Greek framework left to satisfy, no attendance requirement, and no permission to seek. Greece thereby joins Sweden, Norway, the Czech Republic, and Hungary in our coverage\u2019s post-compulsory pattern — with one advantage most of those countries lack: Athens\u2019s British-curriculum schools run A-Levels as core business, so the exam infrastructure sits an hour from most of the population.'},
      {h:'Planning backward from fifteen',p:'The window rewards families who aim at it early. The compulsory years run supplementary — the school enrolment carrying the legal obligation, live Cambridge Mathematics, sciences, and English filling the afternoon the frontistirio culture already reserves — with IGCSE sittings scheduled at Athens centres around age fifteen or sixteen. Then gymnasio ends, the obligation ends with it, and the A-Level phase simplifies to a single calendar: subjects chosen for the university target, two to three years of live teaching in groups of 4-6, sessions booked per exam window. Two supplementary-configured years buy three entirely free ones.'},
      {h:'The comparison every UK-bound family eventually makes',p:'By sixteen, a Greek student aiming at a UK university faces two roads. Road one: three years of lykeio, taught in Greek, aimed at Panhellenic examinations that UK admissions offices must translate. Road two: A-Levels — the qualification UCAS reads natively, in the subjects the target course actually asks for, with predicted grades and references generated by a school that speaks the admissions system\u2019s own language. Both roads are legitimate; families choosing the second have simply noticed where their child is going. And the road home stays open: under the 2024 law, non-state branch campuses of foreign universities have begun opening in Athens, admitting on international qualifications — an emerging way to hold A-Levels and still study in Greece.'},
      {h:'The islands: where the question was never legal',p:'On Crete, across the Ionian, and through the Dodecanese, the post-gymnasio question is not legal but geographic. These islands hold real international communities — FORTH\u2019s globally recruited researchers in Heraklion, two centuries of British families on Corfu, the year-round hospitality economies of Rhodes and Kos — and no international school anywhere in reach: senior international options mean the mainland, a boarding decision, or nothing. For an island teenager past gymnasio, a live A-Level timetable delivered to the kitchen table — with exam-window flights to Athens planned the way islands plan everything — is frequently the strongest academic option in the region, full stop. The recorded library carries the tourism high season; the same-clock summer timezone puts live classes exactly where the afternoon study hours have always been.'},
      {h:'Choosing a provider for a strict-tier country',p:'Ask any virtual school operating in Greece three questions. What does Greek law require during the compulsory years — exactly? How does the supplementary configuration build toward real qualifications? And what happens at fifteen? A provider that answers the first question with anything softer than \u201cschool attendance is compulsory and online programmes do not fulfil it\u201d is blurring the law to make a sale — and a provider that cannot name the optional-lykeio window has not done the homework. Smartious\u2019s answers are this article: the law stated exactly, the supplementary years built toward IGCSE, and the whole pathway planned backward from the window at fifteen.'},
    ],
    faqs:[
      {q:'Is lykeio really optional in Greece?',a:'Yes — compulsory education ends with gymnasio at about fifteen, and upper-secondary school is optional by law. Most students attend; nothing obliges them to. Full-time live A-Levels from fifteen satisfy every requirement.'},
      {q:'What do the compulsory years require?',a:'School attendance, ages 4-15 — a Greek public, private, or licensed international school. Smartious runs supplementary beside that enrolment, building toward IGCSE sittings at Athens centres, then the A-Level phase after gymnasio.'},
      {q:'Can A-Levels lead back to study in Greece?',a:'Greek public universities admit through the Panhellenic examinations in Greek — but under the 2024 law, non-state branch campuses of foreign universities have begun opening in Athens, admitting on international qualifications, alongside a small English-taught offer. UCAS and the Common Application stand open internationally.'},
      {q:'How do island students manage the examinations?',a:'Sittings are confirmed per session at Athens\u2019s authorised centres, with exam-window flights planned well ahead — a few trips a year against three years of live schooling delivered to the island.'},
    ],
    conclusion:'One optional word in Greek law — lykeio — opens a three-year window that changes everything for internationally bound students, and the islands turn that window into the only complete option on the map. Smartious plans the compulsory years toward it and delivers the A-Level phase live, from Kifissia to Karpathos. Book an assessment for your child, or start with our Greece hub and the Heraklion, Corfu, and Rhodes pages.',
  },

